"""Veterinary-resource alternative; never produces a safe-exposure threshold."""
guide('chocolate-for-dogs-calculator-guide','Chocolate for Dogs Calculator Guide — Veterinary Next Steps',
 'Understand dog-chocolate calculator limits, gather the product and exposure details your vet needs, and avoid delaying veterinary advice for an online score.',
 ['chocolate for dogs calculator','dog chocolate toxicity calculator guide','dog ate chocolate what to do','chocolate poisoning in dogs'],'Pet Care Resources',
 'If you suspect your dog ate chocolate, contact your veterinarian or an emergency veterinary service immediately rather than waiting for symptoms or an online score. Keep the packaging and note the possible amount and time. A chocolate for dogs calculator is only an exposure estimate built from assumed product concentrations and body weight; it cannot examine the animal or establish that an exposure is safe. This guide explains what information to prepare and how to interpret the limits of public tools. It contains no safe-dose threshold, poisoning severity classification, home-treatment dose or instructions to induce vomiting. Follow the veterinary professional’s instructions for the actual case.',
 ['Contact veterinary help promptly; do not wait for a calculator verdict.','Keep product packaging and distinguish amount missing from amount definitely eaten.','Record the possible time and the dog’s known weight without inventing precision.','Do not attempt home treatment or induce vomiting without veterinary instruction.','A low modeled exposure is not a guarantee that the animal is safe.'],
 [('Use the calculator question as a prompt to seek help','Searching for a chocolate calculator often happens when an owner is anxious and wants a simple yes-or-no answer. The information needed for a veterinary assessment includes facts a calculator cannot inspect. If chocolate may have been eaten, contact the appropriate veterinary service immediately. A result screen should not become an extra step that postpones that contact.|The reputable resources below place professional advice beside their tools. This SolvePilot guide makes that priority explicit and does not generate a green safe-result badge. It is an information-preparation page, not an emergency triage system. If the animal is unwell, explain that promptly when contacting the emergency service.'),('Keep the original product information','Chocolate type matters to an exposure estimate, and products can contain other ingredients. Preserve the package, ingredient list and remaining product when possible. A description such as dark bar or one cake slice may not establish a precise concentration or chocolate mass. Tell the veterinary professional what is actually known rather than selecting a convenient generic dropdown value.|Brand, package size and ingredient information can be more useful than an unexplained score from a web page. Keep those details available for the conversation or visit. Do not remove the wrapper or discard the remaining item merely because a calculator asks only for the broad chocolate category.'),('Distinguish missing mass from confirmed ingestion','A missing amount can be an upper estimate rather than a measured amount swallowed. Another animal, a dropped piece or an uncertain starting package size can affect the record. Explain the uncertainty instead of turning the entire missing quantity into a falsely exact number. The veterinarian can decide how to use that information.|If you already know the original and remaining masses, preserve both and the unit. Do not spend time constructing a detailed weighing experiment before seeking advice. A rough honest range communicated promptly is more useful than a precise-looking figure obtained by delaying contact.'),('Use body weight with its source and unit','A recent known weight, a veterinary record and a guess are different input qualities. State which one you have and whether it is in kilograms or pounds. Copying pounds into a kilogram field changes the basis substantially. The professional should know when the weight is uncertain instead of receiving only a calculated exposure number.|The related mass-conversion tools are mathematical aids, not toxicity assessors. If a unit is unclear, report the original figure and ask the veterinary service to interpret it. Do not infer an animal’s current weight from breed, a photo or a generic growth chart simply to complete an online form.'),('Explain timing without assigning a safe waiting period','Tell the veterinarian when the chocolate may have been eaten and when it was discovered. If the time is uncertain, give the possible range. The fact that symptoms are not apparent when you find the package does not establish a safe exposure or justify waiting for a later symptom check.|This guide does not provide a countdown after which the animal can be declared safe. Timing and clinical decisions belong to the veterinary assessment. Keep any later change in the animal’s condition available for the professional, and follow the instructions you receive rather than relying on a generic internet monitoring schedule.'),('Understand what an exposure equation omits','A public calculator typically combines animal weight, product mass and an assumed concentration. Its arithmetic can be internally consistent while the concentration or eaten amount is wrong. A generic category does not verify a brand-specific product or the animal’s circumstances. A numerical estimate therefore has limits beyond display rounding.|The veterinary sources identify product variation and the need for professional assessment. This guide does not copy a concentration table into an apparently comprehensive risk tool. It also does not calculate a threshold from body weight and then describe a quantity below it as a safe treat.'),('Avoid unsupervised home-treatment instructions','Do not try to make your dog vomit or give medicines, charcoal or other home remedies based on this page or a calculator result. Veterinary guidance is needed because an intervention can be inappropriate for the actual animal and exposure. Follow the professional’s instructions and use the relevant clinic or emergency process.|A medical-looking formula or a treatment dose copied from a technical manual is not an owner-specific instruction. This guide deliberately contains no treatment calculator or dosing protocol. It directs the user to the people who can assess the animal and decide the next step.'),('Keep public comparison examples fictional','If you are comparing tools after dealing with the immediate veterinary question, use fictional masses and weights rather than exposing an animal owner’s private records. Inspect whether the page identifies its concentration basis, supports the actual product and clearly states that an estimate is not a clinical assessment. Check its privacy and disclaimer before entering identifying details.|A tool’s ability to produce a decimal number does not prove it has verified the exposure. Likewise, a result matching another calculator can mean they share the same assumptions, not that the real situation has been assessed. Preserve the assumptions if you discuss a modeled result with the veterinary professional.'),('Prevent a worksheet from becoming a permission rule','An estimated low exposure is not permission to feed chocolate deliberately. A calculator cannot turn an unsuitable food into a recommended treat or predict how a particular animal will respond. Store chocolate and relevant products securely, and use appropriate pet-feeding guidance for everyday choices.|Keep the purpose of the record clear: it supports communication about a suspected event. It is not a target, a dosing plan or a challenge to approach a threshold. The veterinary next step should remain visible at the top of any exposure worksheet or screenshot.'),('Use a concise handover record','A short record can list product name and package, possible amount, unit, possible ingestion time, known animal weight and any changes you observed. Mark uncertain details as uncertain. Have the remaining product or wrapper ready if the professional asks for it, and follow their advice about whether and where to attend.|This guide is editorial information, not specialist clinical review of a particular animal. It does not collect those records or transmit them to a clinic. Share the actual information directly with the veterinary service through the appropriate channel. Related calculators perform only their disclosed unit arithmetic and never replace that conversation.')],
 'For a fictional recordkeeping example unrelated to safety thresholds, an originally 100 g package with 60 g remaining leaves 40 g unaccounted for. The honest note is “up to forty grams may be missing; actual ingestion uncertain,” together with the package details and possible time. It is not “forty grams definitely eaten” unless that is genuinely known.|This subtraction is not a poisoning assessment. No concentration, animal weight, safe quantity or treatment is inferred from it. In an actual suspected ingestion, contact veterinary help immediately and communicate the facts you already have instead of completing this exercise first.',
 [('Should I wait for symptoms before calling a vet?','No. If you suspect chocolate ingestion, contact a veterinarian or emergency veterinary service promptly rather than waiting for symptoms or a calculator result.'),('Can an online calculator confirm the dog is safe?','No. Its result depends on assumptions and cannot examine the animal, verify the product or replace a case-specific veterinary assessment.'),('What information should I have ready?','Keep the packaging, possible amount and time, known weight and observed condition. State uncertainties instead of inventing exact values.'),('Should I try to induce vomiting?','Do not attempt home treatment or induce vomiting based on this guide. Follow a veterinary professional’s instructions for the actual animal.'),('Does this page calculate a safe amount of chocolate?','No. It provides a veterinary-resource and calculator-limit guide, with no safe-dose threshold or recommendation to feed chocolate.')],
 ['pounds-ounces-converter'],
 [('Cornell veterinary chocolate-toxicity guidance','https://www.vet.cornell.edu/departments-centers-and-institutes/riney-canine-health-center/canine-health-topics/chocolate-toxicity-what-should-i-do-if-my-dog-eats-chocolate','Veterinary institution guidance; prompt professional contact and product/context limits.'),('PDSA chocolate-poisoning guidance','https://www.pdsa.org.uk/pet-help-and-advice/pet-health-hub/conditions/chocolate-poisoning?alias=chocolatepoisoning','Veterinary charity guidance on prompt contact and avoiding home vomiting attempts.'),('ASPCA chocolate exposure resource','https://www.aspca.org/news/what-do-if-your-pet-gets-chocolate','Facts to gather and case-specific veterinary next steps.')],
 'General veterinary-resource guide, not clinical diagnosis, safe-exposure certification, a treatment dose or specialist review of an animal. Suspected ingestion requires prompt veterinary advice. The page does not determine whether home monitoring, decontamination or hospital care is appropriate.')
